   "shape_m": "blockquote",
   "html_text": "PCC (Principle of the Common Cause). For any correlation between two (distinct) events which do not cause each other, there is a common cause that screens them off from each other. Or formally: If distinct events x and y are correlated, i.e., (Correlation) P ( x & y ) ≠ P ( x ) · P ( y ), and they do not cause each other, then their common cause, CC ( x , y ), screens them off from each other, i.e., (Screening Off) P ⟦S⟧ ( x / y ) = P ⟦S⟧ ( x ) P ⟦S⟧ ( y ) ≠ 0 P ⟦S⟧ ( y / x ) = P ⟦S⟧ ( y ) P ⟦S⟧ ( x ) ≠ 0. ⟦P⟧ Accordingly, CC ( x , y ) renders x and y probabilistically independent, and the joint probability of x and y factorizes upon CC ( x , y ): P ⟦S⟧ ( x & y ) = P ⟦S⟧ ( x ) · P ⟦S⟧ ( y ).",
   "md_text": "PCC (Principle of the Common Cause). For any correlation between two (distinct) events which do not cause each other, there is a common cause that screens them off from each other. Or formally: If distinct events x and y are correlated, i.e., > (Correlation) P(x & y) ≠ P(x) · P(y), and they do not cause each other, then their common cause, CC(x,y), screens them off from each other, i.e., > (Screening Off) > > P ⟦S⟧ (x/y) = P ⟦S⟧ (x) P ⟦S⟧ (y) ≠ 0 > --- --- > P ⟦S⟧ (y/x) = P ⟦S⟧ (y) P ⟦S⟧ (x) ≠ 0. Accordingly, CC(x,y) renders x and y probabilistically independent, and the joint probability of x and y factorizes upon CC(x,y): > P ⟦S⟧ (x & y) = P ⟦S⟧ (x) · P ⟦S⟧ (y)."
  }
